{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":21,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.vHat_invChain","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.vHat_invChain","kind":"theorem","line":348,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L348-L375","doc":"**The closed form.**  `V̂ₙ = ((q⁻¹)ⁿ + (−q)ⁿ)/2`.\n\nBecause `|−q| > 1` at the substrate, this grows without bound: only finitely\nmany chain lengths leave `|V̂ₙ| < q`, and the production word's `n = 4` is the\nlast even one.  The 1.95 % margin between `V̂₄` and the `Φ_q` pole is therefore\nthe whole of the headroom — see the section note above for what this does and\ndoes not say about the choice of 4. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.factorProd_replicate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.factorProd_replicate","kind":"theorem","line":333,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L333-L346","doc":"A repeated factor evaluates to a power. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.sigma_mul_sub_h","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.sigma_mul_sub_h","kind":"theorem","line":328,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L328-L331","doc":"`σ(σ − h) = 1` — the skein relation, and the reason `σ − h` is `σ⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.invChain","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.invChain","kind":"def","line":324,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L324-L326","doc":"The `σ⁻¹` chain of length `n`: `σ⁻ⁿ`, since `σ − h = σ⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.vHat_half_h_eq_avg_evals","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.vHat_half_h_eq_avg_evals","kind":"theorem","line":313,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L313-L322","doc":"**The Markov parameter `h/2` is the average of the two eigenvalue\nevaluations.**  This is the conceptual content of `markov_param_eq_half_h`:\ntransposing a shape swaps `q` and `−q⁻¹`, so pairing shapes with their\ntransposes averages the two, and `h/2` is that average. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.isHeckeGen_q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.isHeckeGen_q","kind":"theorem","line":308,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L308-L311","doc":"Both Hecke eigenvalues are roots of the generator's own quadratic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.evalAt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.evalAt","kind":"def","line":302,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L302-L306","doc":"Evaluation of a collapsed element `α·σ + β` at a **scalar** root of the same\nHecke quadratic.  `IsHeckeGen s h` is reused deliberately: `q` and `−q⁻¹` satisfy\n`s² = h·s + 1` for `h = q − q⁻¹` exactly as the generator does, which is what\nmakes the evaluations multiplicative. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.inter_nucleon_vHat","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.inter_nucleon_vHat","kind":"theorem","line":244,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L244-L255","doc":"The single-factor inter-nucleon generator's V̂ is exactly `h·δ/4`.\n\n`δ = 0` is the colour-blind `strict_averaged` convention, and there the\ncontribution vanishes identically — the p–n interface reaches the tower only\nthrough the colour weighting. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.identity_coeff_avg_eq_trace_full","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.identity_coeff_avg_eq_trace_full","kind":"theorem","line":210,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L210-L223","doc":"Appending the **averaged** factor `σ − h/2` and taking the identity\ncoefficient equals appending the **full** factor `σ − h` and taking the Markov\ntrace at `z = h/2`.\n\nThe `h/2` in the trace is not a free choice: it is the Markov parameter forced\nby `markov_param_eq_half_h` for any normalised transpose-symmetric weighting,\nwhich is what the production weight scheme is. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.vHat_eq_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.vHat_eq_iff","kind":"theorem","line":175,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L175-L183","doc":"The pole is hit at exactly one Markov parameter, when the element carries a\nnonzero σ-coefficient. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.vHat_margin","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.vHat_margin","kind":"theorem","line":162,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L162-L173","doc":"**The conditioning statement.**  The margin from the current Markov\nparameter to the one that would put V̂ exactly on the Φ_q pole at `q` is the\nV̂-margin divided by the σ-coefficient.\n\nSo `α` is the amplification from \"how far the convention moved\" to \"how far the\npole moved\": with `α` small the tower is insensitive to the C→D change, and with\n`α` large a 22.7 % shift in `z` can cross the singularity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.vHat_sub_vHat","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.vHat_sub_vHat","kind":"theorem","line":156,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L156-L160","doc":"`vHat` is affine in the Markov parameter, with slope the σ-coefficient.\nChanging convention moves V̂ by `Δz · α` and by nothing else. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.vHat","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.vHat","kind":"def","line":152,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L152-L154","doc":"The Markov trace of a collapsed element `α·σ + β`, with `tr(σ) = z`,\n`tr(1) = 1`.  This is `Vs = z_mark * a + b` in `additive_wedderburn_volumes`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.collapseFold_singleton","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.collapseFold_singleton","kind":"theorem","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L124-L131","doc":"A single factor is returned unchanged: `c·σ + d` is already linear, so the\nfold must be the identity on it.  A sanity check on `collapseStep`'s direction\n— an accumulator that mangled a one-element list would be wrong in a way the\ngeneral theorem could still hide, since `prod_eq_linear` proves the fold agrees\nwith the product without pinning what the fold *is*. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.collapseFold_nil","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.collapseFold_nil","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L121-L122","doc":"The empty product is the identity, as the fold's seed states. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.prod_eq_linear","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.prod_eq_linear","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L97-L119","doc":"**The collapse.**  Any product of linear factors in one Hecke generator is\nlinear in that generator, with coefficients given by `collapseFold`.\n\nThis is the invariant `qou-9ohz`'s O(dim) rewrite of `per_generator_V_hat_3A`\ndepends on.  Proved by induction on the factor list, generalising over the\naccumulator so the fold's running value is available at each step. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.factorProd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.factorProd","kind":"def","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L93-L95","doc":"The product of the factors themselves, in the same order. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.collapseFold","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.collapseFold","kind":"def","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L89-L91","doc":"The accumulator over a list of factors, folded from the identity `1 = 0·σ + 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.collapseStep_spec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.collapseStep_spec","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L78-L87","doc":"`collapseStep` is correct: it computes the product. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.collapseStep","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.collapseStep","kind":"def","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L65-L76","doc":"One step of the accumulator: multiplying `α·σ + β` by `c·σ + d` again\nyields something linear in `σ`, with these coefficients.\n\n    (α σ + β)(c σ + d) = α c σ² + (α d + β c) σ + β d\n                       = α c (h σ + 1) + (α d + β c) σ + β d\n                       = (α (c h + d) + β c) σ + (α c + β d)\n\nThis is the whole optimisation: two ring multiplications and an add per step,\nindependent of the irrep dimension, where the naive route is a `dim × dim`\nmatrix product. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.IsHeckeGen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.IsHeckeGen","kind":"def","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/OneGeneratorHeckeCollapse.lean#L59-L63","doc":"The Hecke quadratic in the form the braid-word accumulator uses:\n`σ² = h·σ + 1`, with `h = q - q⁻¹`.  Carried as a hypothesis rather than\nbuilt from `q`, so the statement holds for any `h` — including the `q = 1`\ndegeneration `h = 0`, where `σ² = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}